  "articleBody": "Before you start tearing down those old kitchen cabinets, a little preparation goes a long way! Use this checklist to make sure you're ready for a smooth removal process. First, completely empty all cabinets and drawers, and clear your countertops. You'll want an empty workspace! Second, identify and shut off all utilities connected to your kitchen. That means turning off the water supply to your sink and dishwasher, and cutting power to any outlets or lighting circuits in the kitchen. Next, gather your tools: you’ll likely need screwdrivers, a utility knife for cutting caulk, a pry bar, a drill, and possibly a reciprocating saw. Don't forget safety gear like gloves and eye protection. Before you start unscrewing, gently cut any caulk or paint lines where the cabinets meet the walls or ceiling. This helps prevent drywall damage. Plan your removal sequence: generally, upper cabinets come down first, then base cabinets. Remember, if any cabinets are attached to plumbing or electrical, or if countertops are heavy, a professional like HH Remodeling LLC can handle these complex elements safely and efficiently.",
